## Black Sun

Black Sun is a galaxy-spanning criminal syndicate whose influence extends through the Core Worlds, the Outer Rim, and many major trade routes. Unlike organizations that openly claim territory, Black Sun operates through concealed networks of intermediaries, local gangs, corrupt officials, private security firms, merchants, and legitimate corporations. Many operatives may complete assignments without knowing who ultimately benefits from their work.

The syndicate profits from smuggling, extortion, information trading, illegal technology, protection agreements, theft, and control of black-market distribution networks. It has maintained operations on worlds such as Mustafar and Ord Mantell, while using urban centers like Coruscant and Nar Shaddaa to move credits, intelligence, and contraband. Black Sun does not rely on a single permanent capital; regional leaders manage their own territories while remaining connected to a wider command structure.

Black Sun prefers secrecy, leverage, and economic pressure over open warfare. Contracts are usually delivered through intermediaries and may hide the identity of the true client. Payments can be extremely high, but assignments often contain incomplete information or competing objectives. The syndicate’s relationship with the Guild is officially hostile, although individual brokers and hunters may secretly work for Black Sun when the reward outweighs the risk.

## Bounty Hunters Guild

The Bounty Hunters Guild is a professional network that connects clients, contract brokers, and independent bounty hunters across the galaxy. It is not a government or standing military force. Instead, it provides a recognized system for distributing contracts, verifying targets, recording completed assignments, transferring rewards, and maintaining the professional reputation of its members.

Local Guild chapters are commonly managed by contractors or bounty brokers who receive requests from governments, corporations, private citizens, and less reputable clients. They distribute bounty pucks, target records, tracking devices, and payment conditions to qualified hunters. Important trade worlds and frontier settlements may maintain Guild offices inside cantinas, administrative buildings, fortified compounds, or private communication centers.

Members remain independent but are expected to follow Guild rules. A hunter must respect the stated condition of a contract, protect confidential information, provide acceptable proof of completion, and avoid interfering with another member’s registered claim. Violations may result in withheld payment, damaged reputation, expulsion, or an authorized bounty against the offender. Within the project, the Guild acts as the central professional organization supporting the contract market and is naturally allied with its registered hunters.

## Corellian Engineering Guild

The Corellian Engineering Guild is a professional federation of shipwrights, mechanics, designers, navigators, systems specialists, and industrial contractors based primarily in the Corellian system. It represents independent workshops, technical schools, repair yards, freight operators, and engineering families that do not wish to operate under the direct control of a single corporation.

The Guild maintains major chapters near Coronet City, civilian spaceports, orbital construction platforms, and regional shipyards. Its members work on freighters, transports, navigation systems, hyperdrives, droids, station infrastructure, and specialized vessels built for difficult frontier conditions. Guild certification is widely respected because licensed engineers must meet strict standards of safety, documentation, and technical competence.

The organization is politically neutral and prefers commercial stability, open trade routes, and protection of technical knowledge. It regularly hires hunters for prototype recovery, engineer escort, stolen-ship investigation, industrial security, and the retrieval of equipment from abandoned facilities. The Guild maintains a friendly relationship with the Bounty Hunters Guild, although it refuses contracts involving unnecessary destruction of valuable ships, infrastructure, or technical archives.

## Frontier Worlds Union

The Frontier Worlds Union is a decentralized political and economic alliance formed by independent settlements, small planetary governments, trade councils, and remote colonies in the Outer Rim. Its members cooperate because major galactic governments often cannot provide reliable security, infrastructure, or emergency assistance across distant frontier systems.

The Union has no permanent capital. Representatives meet through a rotating assembly hosted by important member worlds and regional trade stations. Each planet retains its own laws and government, while the Union coordinates hyperspace beacons, rescue services, anti-piracy patrols, medical shipments, reconstruction projects, and negotiations with larger powers. Worlds such as Lothal, Nevarro, and other independent frontier communities may participate without surrendering their sovereignty.

Union culture emphasizes local independence, mutual defense, practical cooperation, and suspicion toward distant central authorities. Its security forces are small and usually consist of planetary marshals, volunteer pilots, local defense units, and converted civilian vessels. The Union frequently posts legitimate Guild contracts for convoy escort, missing-person searches, settlement protection, disaster response, and the investigation of raider activity. Its relationship with the Guild is friendly, though payment may depend on several member governments approving shared expenses.

## Hutt Cartel

The Hutt Cartel is a broad alliance of powerful Hutt families whose commercial and criminal influence extends throughout Hutt Space and deep into the Outer Rim. It is not a single unified corporation. Each family controls its own territory, businesses, clients, and armed retainers while participating in councils that settle disputes and establish common policy when shared interests are threatened.

Nal Hutta serves as the traditional political center of Hutt power, while Nar Shaddaa is its largest commercial marketplace. Hutt influence also extends to Tatooine, remote spaceports, gambling centers, shipping companies, mining operations, and settlements positioned along smuggling routes. The Cartel earns credits through transportation, entertainment, debt collection, protection agreements, black-market trade, and control of local infrastructure.

Hutt law is based on contracts, tribute, family authority, and personal obligation rather than equal public legislation. Agreements are usually respected when properly witnessed and profitable, but their wording may be deliberately unfavorable to weaker participants. The Cartel maintains a neutral relationship with the Guild because Hutts are frequent clients and employers. Hunters who respect territorial authority and deliver results can build long careers, while those who break an agreement may find their Guild reputation irrelevant inside Hutt-controlled space.

## Imperial Remnant

The Imperial Remnant consists of military officers, governors, intelligence personnel, scientists, industrial administrators, and loyalists who survived the collapse of the Galactic Empire. It is not a single unified government. Individual commanders control isolated garrisons, hidden bases, warships, research facilities, and remaining supply networks while competing for resources and political authority.

Remnant forces operate from abandoned Imperial installations, remote mining worlds, fortified cities, and mobile fleets. Their resources may include stormtroopers, security droids, intelligence archives, prototype weapons, old naval vessels, and equipment recovered from former Imperial depots. Some groups attempt to rebuild Imperial power, while others focus only on survival, local control, or secret research.

Imperial law remains authoritarian and military in character. Commanders may impose curfews, restricted zones, forced requisitions, identification checks, and immediate detention without civilian review. The Remnant is officially hostile to the Guild, although its agents frequently hire independent hunters through concealed identities and unofficial intermediaries. Such contracts often offer rare technology or unusually large rewards but may be illegal under New Republic law and connected to wider political operations.

## Mandalorian Covert

A Mandalorian Covert is a hidden community established by Mandalorian survivors who cannot safely live openly. Coverts usually occupy underground halls, abandoned industrial sectors, remote fortresses, caves, or concealed settlements. They are organized around clans, foundlings, warriors, craftspeople, and religious or cultural leaders rather than conventional political institutions.

The central figure in many traditional coverts is an Armorer who preserves the knowledge required to forge beskar and maintain Mandalorian customs. Members contribute resources to the community and are expected to protect its location. Armor, signets, family history, personal oaths, and the adoption of foundlings carry deep cultural importance. Individual warriors may work as bounty hunters, escorts, mercenaries, or pilots to obtain supplies for the covert.

A covert follows the Creed and the decisions of its recognized elders. Internal disputes are resolved according to tradition, clan authority, and personal responsibility rather than outside law. The faction maintains a cautious but generally friendly relationship with the Guild. Mandalorians value clear terms and completed obligations, but they will place the survival of the covert, the protection of foundlings, and the recovery of beskar above any commercial contract.

## Mining Guild

The Mining Guild is a large industrial network involved in the extraction, processing, and transportation of natural resources throughout the galaxy. It operates mines, gas refineries, ore crawlers, drilling facilities, cargo stations, and worker settlements on remote planets, moons, asteroids, and uninhabited planetoids. During the Imperial era, it cooperated closely with the Empire and received access to military equipment and protected markets.

Guild operations are highly mobile and can be established wherever profitable resources are discovered. Large extraction vehicles strip terrain for minerals and energy sources, while fortified refineries process materials for transport to industrial worlds. The organization employs engineers, miners, surveyors, pilots, security droids, armed guards, and local administrators. Its remote facilities often function as isolated company towns under the authority of a mining director.

Mining Guild regulations prioritize production, ownership rights, and protection of industrial equipment. Worker safety and environmental preservation vary greatly between individual operations. The organization maintains a neutral relationship with the Bounty Hunters Guild and regularly posts contracts for survey-team escort, cargo recovery, sabotage investigation, missing-worker searches, and security at remote facilities. Hunters should carefully examine whether a contract involves legitimate protection or the suppression of local resistance to a mining claim.

## New Republic

The New Republic is the democratic government created by the Rebel Alliance after the fall of the Galactic Empire. It restored representative institutions, re-established the Galactic Senate, and attempted to replace military occupation with planetary self-government and civil law. Member worlds retain significant autonomy and send representatives to participate in galactic legislation.

Rather than maintaining Coruscant as a permanent center of power, the New Republic adopted a rotating capital system. Its institutions include the Senate, civilian ministries, courts, diplomatic services, correctional facilities, customs agencies, and regional security patrols. After years of war, the government sharply reduced its armed forces, leaving many distant Outer Rim systems with only occasional patrols and limited emergency support.

The New Republic requires legal warrants, documented evidence, clear jurisdiction, and humane treatment of captured targets. It usually hires Guild members for fugitive recovery, witness protection, transport security, missing-person investigations, and assistance in regions where official forces are unavailable. Its relationship with the Guild is allied but regulated. Unauthorized executions, concealed criminal clients, and contracts issued by Imperial remnants or syndicates may result in prosecution even when accepted through a Guild broker.

## Pyke Syndicate

The Pyke Syndicate is a powerful criminal organization specializing in the extraction, transportation, refinement, and distribution of spice. Its leadership is based on Oba Diah, where a fortified stronghold serves as the political and administrative center of Pyke power. The Syndicate maintains an especially strong presence on Kessel and along the hyperspace routes connecting mining worlds with major markets.

Pyke operations include mines, prisons, cargo depots, armored transports, security forces, smugglers, and agreements with local criminal organizations. Spice shipments travel from production sites to refineries and distribution centers on worlds such as Coruscant, Ord Mantell, Nar Shaddaa, and Tatooine. The Syndicate prefers to control every stage of the supply chain, allowing it to influence prices and punish competitors who interfere with established routes.

The Pykes are pragmatic and usually avoid conflict that threatens long-term profit, but they respond severely to theft, unpaid debts, lost shipments, and broken agreements. Contracts may involve cargo escort, the recovery of stolen spice, elimination of rival routes, or the search for missing couriers. The Syndicate’s relationship with the Guild is hostile, although individual Pyke bosses frequently employ hunters through intermediaries. Payment is usually precise when a contract succeeds, but failure may create a permanent debt rather than simply ending the assignment.
